WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.240
Reelin is needed for neurons to take their proper places in the developing brain.

2
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.400
A mouse without this protein reels as it walks.

3
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.800
Its cerebellum is small, and its cortical layers are disorganized and roughly

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.300
reversed. The problem is placement: neurons are made on schedule, but settle in the

5
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.300
wrong order.

6
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:27.673
Normally, neurons climb from the ventricular zone toward the surface.

7
00:00:28.493 --> 00:00:30.873
The first ones form a thin sheet, the preplate.

8
00:00:31.673 --> 00:00:33.913
The next wave enters that sheet and splits it.

9
00:00:34.693 --> 00:00:36.533
Each new wave then climbs past the last.

10
00:00:37.453 --> 00:00:38.973
Early-born neurons end up deep.

11
00:00:39.913 --> 00:00:41.953
Late-born neurons end up near the surface.

12
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:47.887
Birthdating showed the same classes of neurons, born on the same schedule, in reeler

13
00:00:47.887 --> 00:00:50.427
mice. But the preplate fails to split.

14
00:00:51.307 --> 00:00:54.527
Later waves tend to stop beneath the neurons already there.

15
00:00:55.307 --> 00:00:59.327
Early-born neurons tend to sit near the surface, and late-born neurons deep.

16
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.007
Reelin is needed for normal layering.

17
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.160
How does the signal get inside a neuron?

18
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.940
Reelin binds two lipoprotein receptors, ApoER2 and VLDLR.

19
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.300
Inside, the kinases Fyn and Src add phosphate to the adaptor Dab1.

20
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.620
Phosphorylated Dab1 recruits effectors.

21
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.880
Cullin 5 then helps mark it for destruction, limiting the signal.

22
00:01:24.883 --> 00:01:28.383
Remove a runner from a relay and the baton never arrives.

23
00:01:29.403 --> 00:01:33.963
Mice without Reelin, without Dab1, or without both receptors have matching defects.

24
00:01:34.863 --> 00:01:36.243
That suggests one pathway.

25
00:01:37.243 --> 00:01:42.123
Biochemistry gives the order: brains without Reelin have more Dab1, but less

26
00:01:42.123 --> 00:01:43.623
phosphorylated Dab1.

27
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:46.067
Where does Reelin come from?

28
00:01:46.847 --> 00:01:51.787
In the developing cortex, Cajal-Retzius cells secrete it in the marginal zone, at

29
00:01:51.787 --> 00:01:56.507
the surface. That puts the signal near the place where each new wave of neurons

30
00:01:56.507 --> 00:01:57.507
should settle.

31
00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.490
On one view, Reelin acts at specific steps of migration.

32
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:08.170
Signaling through Dab1 and Rap1 has been linked to orienting multipolar neurons and

33
00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.890
to adhesion through N-cadherin.

34
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:16.030
In the final step, somal translocation, a neuron anchors its leading process in the

35
00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.950
marginal zone and pulls its cell body up, without glial support.

36
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:26.110
The effect depends on the stage: orientation, adhesion, and that final movement help

37
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.390
neurons take their places.
